rsi strategy: avoid edges early, aim for corners late, and control mobility.\n\nHere, (C,8) is a corner and currently capturable. Once taken, it cannot be flipped back. So it's highly valuable.\n\nAlso, after taking C8, it supports future plays on B8, D8, C7, etc.\n\nWhereas (E,2) is strong but in a contested area — opponent may respond strongly.\n\nMoreover, flipping D2 and E3 can also be done by other moves — but corner C8 is only capturable now via (C,8).\n\nIs there any other way to take C8 later? Only if someone plays there — but if I don’t take it, opponent might not allow it.\n\nSince it’s available now, and corners are critical, it should be prioritized.\n\nBut let’s confirm if (C,8) is indeed valid and flips:\n\nPlace * at C8.\nCheck directions:\n- South: D8=@ — continue to E8=* → yes, sandwich, so flip D8.\n- Other directions: west: C7=. — no.\n- North: B8=. — no.\n- East: D8 already considered.\n- Diagonals: B7=*, so C8 to B7 — diagonal, but B7=*, C8=new*, no @ in between — no flip.\n- Southwest: D7=*, so C8 to D7 — not aligned directly? C8 to D7 is diagonal? C8, D7 — yes, southeast? No: C8 to D7 is south-southeast? Coordinates: C8 is col C row 8, D7 is col D row 7 — that’s southeast diagonal. So C8 → D7=*, adjacent — no @, so no flip.\n\nSo only flip is D8 — one piece.\n\nBut corner is worth it.\n\nNow, (C,7) flips two pieces and is closer to center.\n\nBut (C,8) takes a corner.\n\nIn Reversi, corners are generally preferred over immediate gains.\n\nAlso, (C,1) is a pseudo-corner but not actual corner — C1 is not corner; A1, A8, H1, H8 are corners. C1 is just edge.\n\nSimilarly, E2 is central edge.\n\nAnother factor: mobility. Taking C8 reduces opponent’s options and secures a safe area.\n\nMoreover, looking at the board, the top-left (A1) is still empty — but no one can play there yet.\n\nAfter reviewing, (C,8) seems strategically best.\n\nBut wait — is there a risk? After playing C8, opponent might play B8 or C7, but I can respond.\n\nAlternatively, (E